The [original incident](/kb/incidents/4471/) called this one known parser bug.
The rendered evidence shows two causes. Engineering has since closed #4471 with two
different outcomes: the parser was fixed; the hint was not fixed because booking-v3
was retired. Keep those outcomes separate from the report counts.

<Callout kind="warn">
Report counts cover the export dated 4 October 2026. Resolution status comes from the
course's separate engineering update dated 5 October. Rebuild against a new export
before quoting current totals; the review date does not make the data newer.
</Callout>

## Engineering outcome — 5 October 2026

The [course's engineering update](https://hq.mega.dev/challenges/what-it-shows#engineering-update)
reports **#4471 closed 05 oct**:

| Cause | Engineering outcome | Our ticket |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Parser | **Fixed** — `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales` | [Parser issue #1](https://github.com/Julian-Guillo/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1): completed |
| Hint | **Won't fix** — booking-v3 form retired | [Hint issue #2](https://github.com/Julian-Guillo/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2): not planned |

Retiring a form is a different resolution from fixing its hint. Do not call the hint
fixed, or infer that every anomalous report was explained. The update is the source of
these statuses; no product patch or reproduction environment was supplied.

## Count by cause, not intake label

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The table preserves the export's original ticket ownership, including its “no ticket
of its own” label. The linked engineering tickets and their outcomes are above.
The table executes `causes.ts` against tenant locale and form configuration. The ticket
export has overlapping pages: count each report ID once, then select reports merged
into #4471. `meta.intakeTag` is an intake guess based on browser locale, not the cause.
The “tagged as” column exposes that disagreement.

- **Parser:** a day-first tenant's date reaches the shared month-first parser and its
  day and month are exchanged. This includes app and email reports, and form reports
  from booking-v2. For example, R-41200 reports 7 April becoming 4 July.
- **Hint:** booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` for day-first tenants. People follow the hint,
  but the form reads the value in the tenant's date order. This affects form reports
  from Alder Physio, Copper Studio and Pale Harbour. R-41230 belongs here even though
  intake called it parser. R-41205 belongs to parser even though intake called it hint.

The parser needed to respect the input's declared date order. The separate hint
mismatch needed consistent form instructions or retirement of the affected form.
Engineering reports the former as fixed and the latter as retired. Test the parser's
remaining paths separately; matching symptoms do not prove the same fix.

### Reports that need another look

The table's one-day-move footnote comes from `desk-notes.json`. Those IDs remain counted
where the running classifier places them; they are not an extra set to add to the total.
Their bodies do not establish a day/month swap, and tenant attribution alone does not
prove their root cause:

- R-41202: Tuesday 12 May versus Monday 11 May.
- R-41209: every June booking is one day off.
- R-41236: the invite reportedly arrived before the booking was made.
- R-41344: the same meeting shows Tuesday in Lisbon and Monday in Toronto.
- R-41620: Tuesday 6 October shows as Monday; the reporter says they stopped reporting.

Keep these reports visible for investigation. Collect original input, stored date,
rendered date, timezone and delivery timestamps before asserting another defect or fix.

## The queue stayed active

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Received and merged are different clocks. The July merge spike is a desk action,
not the date every problem began. Reports arrived from April through October and
continued after the bulk merge. “Known” and “fix queued” did not mean resolved;
resolution is established separately by the 5 October engineering update above.

## Replace subject-only triage

[Dedupe decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject/) and the
[triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe/) were reviewed in June 2024. Their subject-only rule
contradicts the running explorer. Preserve the accepted decision as history; propose a
new decision to supersede it rather than silently rewriting the old record.

<DedupeExplorer />

Read the body and resolve the tenant, locale, form version and channel. Merge only when
the evidence supports the same cause. Different subjects can share a cause; the same
subject can hide different causes. An unknown tenant is unplaced, not evidence of a
duplicate. Keep uncertain reports separate until investigated.

## Sources and maintenance

All evidence is in this export: `reports-4471.json`, the tenant directory,
`desk-notes.json`, `causes.ts`, the rendered tables, timeline and explorer. No product
implementation or live queue was supplied, so the proposed fixes still need reproduction
against the product. Revisit this review when the desk supplies new reports or tenant
configuration, and track each cause's own resolution rather than closing all of #4471.

